# useCropperInstance

Creates a `CropperInstance`: the core's state machine (`AbstractCropperInstance`) wired to Svelte reactivity. It holds the cropper state, the transitions and the interactions, and has every state-changing method (`moveCoordinates`, `zoomImage`, `setState`, …). It doesn't load images or measure anything. [`useAbstractCropper`](/docs/hooks/useAbstractCropper) builds on it and adds that part.

Use it directly when you want the cropping logic without the standard components, for example to drive a custom canvas or WebGL renderer.

## Signature

```ts
function useCropperInstance<Settings extends CropperInstanceSettings, Instance = unknown>(
	props: () => AbstractCropperInstanceParameters<Settings> &
		AbstractCropperInstanceCallbacks<Instance> & {
			settings?: CropperInstanceSettingsProp<Settings>;
		}
): CropperInstance<Settings, Instance>;

type CropperStateHook = ReturnType<typeof useCropperInstance>;
```

## Parameters

`props` is a **getter**. The instance calls it every time it needs settings or callbacks, so it always works with current values.

| Option        | Type                                         | Default | Description                                                                                       |
| ------------- | -------------------------------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `settings`    | `CropperInstanceSettingsProp<Settings>`      | `{}`    | The core [settings](/docs/concept/settings). Passed through `createDefaultSettings`.              |
| `transitions` | `boolean \| { timingFunction?, duration? }`  | `true`  | Whether programmatic changes are flagged for animation, and with which timing.                    |
| `postProcess` | `PostprocessFunction \| PostprocessFunction[]` |         | Functions that adjust the state after each operation.                                             |
| `*Algorithm`  | core algorithm functions                     |         | Replace a default modifier (`setCoordinatesAlgorithm`, `transformImageAlgorithm`, …).             |
| `getInstance` | `() => Instance \| null`                     |         | Returns the value that callbacks receive. **Callbacks only fire while it returns something truthy.** |
| `onChange`, `onUpdate`, `onMove`, `onResize`, … | `(instance: Instance) => void` | | The core callbacks.                                                                |

Before the settings reach the core, two defaults are added: `imageRestriction` becomes `ImageRestriction.fitArea` and `transformImage` becomes `{ adjustStencil: true }`. If you pass your own `transformImage`, it replaces that object rather than merging into it.

## Return value

A `CropperInstance`. Its main methods:

- **Reading**: `getState()`, `getCoordinates()`, `getVisibleArea()`, `getTransforms()`, `getStencilCoordinates()`, `getSettings()`, `getTransitions()`, `getInteractions()`, `hasInteractions()`, `isConsistent()`.
- **Lifecycle**: `reset(boundary, image)` creates the state for an image and a boundary size, `clear()` removes it, and `setBoundary(boundary)` adapts it to a new size.
- **Changing the state**: `setState`, `setCoordinates`, `setVisibleArea`, `moveCoordinates`/`moveCoordinatesEnd`, `resizeCoordinates`/`resizeCoordinatesEnd`, `transformImage`/`transformImageEnd`, `zoomImage`, `moveImage`, `rotateImage`, `flipImage`, `reconcileState`, `startTransitions`.

The instance keeps its data in a `$state.raw` field, so every reading method is reactive: use them in markup or `$derived` and they update on each change. The methods are pre-bound, so `onclick={instance.clear}` works.

## When to call it

Usually once in a component's `<script>`, where the getter can read the component's props. It creates no effects, so it also works outside components, for example in a `.svelte.ts` module.

## Example

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
	import {
		useCropperInstance,
		type CropperImage,
		type CropperInstanceSettings
	} from 'svelte-advanced-cropper';

	let { image, aspectRatio = 1 }: { image: CropperImage; aspectRatio?: number } = $props();

	const instance = useCropperInstance<CropperInstanceSettings, { id: string }>(() => ({
		settings: { aspectRatio: { minimum: aspectRatio, maximum: aspectRatio } },
		// Callbacks only fire when getInstance returns something truthy.
		getInstance: () => ({ id: 'my-cropper' }),
		onChange: (owner) => console.log(owner.id, instance.getCoordinates())
	}));

	const coordinates = $derived(instance.getCoordinates());
</script>

<button onclick={() => instance.reset({ width: 400, height: 300 }, image)}>Reset</button>
<button onclick={() => instance.zoomImage(1.5)}>Zoom in</button>
<pre>{JSON.stringify(coordinates)}</pre>
```

<Admonition type="note">

The core reconciles the state with the settings only when asked. `useAbstractCropper` does it automatically after settings change. With a bare instance, call `instance.reconcileState()` yourself, for example in an `$effect` that reads the settings you pass in.

</Admonition>

## Differences from React

- React stores the data in a plain field and forces a re-render of the component on every change. Here the data is a `$state.raw` signal, so only the parts of the UI that read it update.
- React's getter could see the props of the previous render. Here it reads current values.
- `CropperInstance` is exported, so you can name the type or subclass it. Upstream only exposed it through `CropperStateHook`.
